          "line": 68,
          "diff_hunk": "@@ -65,6 +65,9 @@ class GcsReadChannelTest {\n   private static final String TEST_PROJECT_ID = \"test-project-id\";\n   private static final String TEST_BUCKET = \"test-bucket\";\n   private static final String TEST_OBJECT = \"test-object\";\n+  private static final String TEST_DECRYPTION_KEY = \"dGVzdC1lbmNyeXB0aW9uLWtleQ==\";",
          "body": "![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)\n\nThe test decryption key \"dGVzdC1lbmNyeXB0aW9uLWtleQ==\" decodes to \"test-encryption-key\", which is only 19 bytes (152 bits). Customer-supplied encryption keys (CSEK) in Google Cloud Storage must be 256-bit (32-byte) AES keys. If client-side validation for key length is introduced or if these tests are run against a real GCS backend/emulator that validates key length, the tests will fail. It is highly recommended to use a valid 256-bit (32-byte) Base64-encoded key.\n\n```suggestion\n  private static final String TEST_DECRYPTION_KEY = \"MDEyMzQ1Njc4OTAxMjM0NTY3ODkwMTIzNDU2Nzg5MDE=\";\n```",
